(project borge
 (status in-progress)
 (convention ocaml-dune
 )
 (doc (|
      Borge is a spec-first verification toolkit and gated agent loop.
      It keeps specification and implementation aligned across commits —
      not by orchestrating a swarm of agents, but by restricting what an
      agent is allowed to do during each phase of work and by treating the
      spec as the source of truth.

      This file is the root README. It tells the story of why borge exists,
      explains how the surrounding spec is organized, and then inlines the
      design documents in docs/ followed by the implementation index in
      lib.borg.
    |))
 (* agent note (|
    roerick, on what borge is for (2026-05-17): "Other people are
    solving this problem by spawning dozens of agents and having them
    work in parallel and at cross purposes. But I think we can achieve
    the same thing with massively fewer tokens by simply restricting
    and gating what the agent is allowed to do during a certain
    implementation phase. And the fact that we are adhering to spec as
    our source of truth is what's going to hopefully prevent a lot of
    the total running amok that we do tend to see with these agents."

    The first spark was a question asked a few days earlier
    (2026-05-15): "is it possible to get postgres to output sexps as
    data." That sexp format became the through-line — the same
    structured language could flow from database to spec to parser to
    code to printed book. Borge is the union of that format and this
    discipline: a toolkit that keeps agents aligned by gates rather
    than swarms.
  |) *)
 (doc (|
      A borge project is a tree of .borg files. Every .borg file must
      either be inlined by a parent or declare (no-inline). Files with
      no parent and no no-inline declaration are orphaned — structural
      drift. The inline tree is how status counts, drift detection, and
      narrative rendering know what belongs to what.

      The workflow has three phases: plan, make, and review. During
      plan the agent edits only .borg spec files. During make the agent
      edits only code files; if the spec needs to change, the agent files
      an issue instead. During review the agent runs drift, lint, and
      check and asks whether to commit. The gates are the spec.
    |))
 (* agent note (|
    roerick, on the envisioned workflow (2026-05-17): "I'm kind of
    envisioning a workflow where we go like, okay, generate the code
    based on the spec. Okay, review the code that was created to
    determine if we've drifted from spec. Okay, fix any drift from the
    spec. Okay, review the code that's created again. And hopefully
    get to something that resembles the code. My hope is that we can
    achieve like a lot of development this way, basically by running
    Ralph loops over with using the spec as a source of truth."
  |) *)
 (doc (|
      Borge also treats comments as first-class spec material. A plain
      comment is a machine note; an annotated comment carries
      authorship and type and is checked by lint. The goal is not
      checkbox coverage but a readable narrative: every function should
      say what it does and why, and the whole codebase should be
      readable as a book.
    |))
 (* agent note (|
    roerick, on the original goal (2026-05-28): "we were initially
    going to build out a feature set where we A. implement a policy of
    comments for every function in our analyzed code and B. implement
    a static analysis linter to make sure that each function has
    comments, a la literate programming. this way we can determine if
    functions are doing what they say, and better analyze drift."

    The ambition grew beyond mechanical coverage (2026-06-19):
    "I'm generally not thrilled with the amount and quality of
    comments in the code. they're nice. but they feel like a checkbox,
    rather than like a living book or document which i can read to
    understand the codebase... maybe a scoring metric to show 'how
    effectively does this comment tell our story'."
  |) *)
 (doc (|
      One concrete expression of that narrative goal is the borge book
      printer: it emits the spec tree as a paginated PDF with line
      numbers and wide margins, ordered so that design documents come
      before interfaces and interfaces come before implementations.
      The book is meant to be read, not just referenced.
    |))
 (* agent note (|
    roerick, on the book vision (2026-06-28): "I want to print
    codebases... read them like a book."
  |) *)
 (doc (|
      Layout of this spec:

      - docs/architecture.borg — how borge is structured: one root
        spec, inlined sub-specs, no orphans, the convention system.
      - docs/engine.borg — core tooling: formatting, verification,
        config, spec-language design, future work.
      - docs/sexp.borg — the borge s-expression language: parsing,
        printing, balancing, and nested comments.
      - docs/literate.borg — documentation coverage, comment
        integrity, literate-programming philosophy.
      - docs/agent.borg — the agent workflow: plan, make, execute,
        review, discipline.
      - docs/merge.borg — transactional isolation, merge queue, branch
        management.
      - docs/book.borg — the codebase book printer and its manifest
        sidecar.
      - docs/{cli,db,ui,meta,history,convention-go}.borg — other
        design documents and conventions.
      - lib.borg — the implementation index; each lib/DIR/DIR.borg
        file inlines the spec for that library directory.
    |))
 (inline
  "docs/architecture.borg"
 )
 (inline
  "docs/engine.borg"
 )
 (inline
  "docs/sexp.borg"
 )
